": {"margin": -0.5, "lean": -4.5, "environment": 4.0, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": 4.5, "sigma_race": 5.3, "sigma_total": 5.73, "baseline_margin": 5.57, "analyst_adjustment": -0.07, "key_factors": ["Rob Sand leads every recent poll, with averages clustering around D+6 to D+8", "Open seat in an R-leaning state caps the Democratic edge short of safe", "Prediction markets and most raters converge on a clear but not commanding Democratic edge", "Live-voter polls run tighter (+1 to +5) than registered-voter surveys"], "rationale": "Every September poll shows Sand ahead, including surveys from Republican-sponsored firms, and markets price his win odds in the low 80s; but Iowa's Republican baseline and a tighter live-voter polling picture keep this at Likely rather than Safe Democratic.", "watch": "If final-week polling narrows toward the +1 to +4 live-voter results, or Lahn consolidates Republican voters in the campaign's last stretch, the race could slip back toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Kim Reynolds (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Iowa's governor race is an open seat now that Republican Kim Reynolds is retiring. State Auditor Rob Sand, the Democratic nominee, faces Republican Zach Lahn, with independent Sondra Wilson also on the ballot. Polls since August consistently show Sand ahead, with margins ranging from three to twelve points; recent survey averages put him up roughly six to eight points, and even Republican-sponsored pollsters show him leading by about four.\n\nRating outlets mostly call the race Lean Democratic, with one Tilt D, one Tossup, and one Likely D. Prediction markets price Sand's chances around 82 to 84 percent. The state's Republican lean keeps this short of safe, and live-voter surveys show a tighter race than registered-voter polls. Sand's statewide name recognition and busy town-hall tour underpin his edge, while Lahn is still building his profile.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-2